================================================================================
What is redistributed, and what is not
================================================================================

The distinction matters more here than the component list does, because narwhal
has two very different delivery modes and only one of them redistributes.

  A NORMAL INSTALL redistributes almost nothing. scripts/cluster/ and the GitOps
  charts reference upstream images and charts by name and version; the cluster
  pulls them from ghcr.io, registry.k8s.io, quay.io and docker.io at install
  time, directly from their publishers. Referencing software is not distributing
  it, so those components arrive under their own terms with no obligation
  passing through this repository.

  THE AIR-GAP BUNDLE redistributes all of it. scripts/airgap/ assembles ~105
  container images, ~27 Helm charts, OS packages and binaries into a single
  directory that is carried across the gap and handed over as one artifact. That
  is distribution, and every component's license applies to whoever hands over
  the bundle.

  THIS REPOSITORY vendors one upstream chart, under
  gitops/charts/kubernetes-dashboard/, which therefore travels with every clone.
  Its attribution is in that directory's own NOTICE.

================================================================================
Air-gap bundle: terms that are not "just Apache-2.0"
================================================================================

The bundle's per-component licenses are recorded in
scripts/airgap/lib/component-licenses.tsv and emitted into the CycloneDX SBOM
that 08-generate-sbom.sh writes to <bundle>/sbom.cdx.json. Each row was resolved
from the upstream project's own license, not inferred.

83 of 100 image repositories are Apache-2.0. The rest are the reason to read the
file rather than assume:

  AGPL-3.0
    grafana, loki, loki-canary, tempo
    Network interaction counts as distribution under AGPL: users who reach these
    services over a network must be offered the corresponding source. Shipping
    them inside a bundle makes that the distributor's obligation, not Grafana's.

  RSAL-2.0 OR SSPL-1.0 OR AGPL-3.0-only
    redis (8.x, upstream image)
    Redis 8 is tri-licensed and RSALv2/SSPLv1 are source-available, NOT OSI open
    source; RSALv2 forbids offering the software as a managed service to third
    parties.
    This is not a component narwhal chooses. Argo CD's upstream install
    manifest deploys `argocd-redis` from public.ecr.aws/docker/library/redis,
    and the bundle picks it up from the running cluster. It is the only
    upstream Redis in the bundle — Harbor's cache is redis-photon
    (goharbor/harbor, Apache-2.0) and is unaffected. Anyone who cannot accept
    RSALv2/SSPLv1 terms should repoint argocd-redis at Valkey (BSD-3-Clause),
    which is already in the bundle for other uses.

  MPL-2.0
    openbao, vault-k8s
    File-level copyleft: modified files stay MPL, the rest of the work does not.

  GPL-2.0
    busybox, frr
    Distribution carries a source-offer obligation. FRR is per-file SPDX and is
    predominantly GPL-2.0-or-later with LGPL and BSD parts.

Anyone redistributing a bundle is responsible for these, and the SBOM exists so
that responsibility is discoverable rather than a surprise found later.

Two limits on what that map can tell you, stated here because an inventory that
quietly under-reports is worse than one that admits its edges:

  IMAGE-LEVEL, NOT PACKAGE-LEVEL. Each row is the license of the software the
  image packages, taken from that project's own repository. It is not the
  license of everything inside the image filesystem. An Alpine-based image also
  contains musl (MIT), busybox (GPL-2.0) and other OS packages;
  ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/postgresql contains PostgreSQL itself (PostgreSQL
  License). For per-file resolution, run syft against the OCI layouts under
  <bundle>/oci/ and attach the results as nested BOMs — 08-generate-sbom.sh
  makes the same point about digests.

  THE IMAGE LIST IS A SNAPSHOT. scripts/airgap/images.txt is regenerated from a
  live cluster, so a component that was not running at that moment is missing
  from it, and therefore from the bundle and its SBOM. Falco is the current
  example: its chart is saved, its images are not listed. Treat a missing
  component as an incomplete inventory, not as evidence it is absent.

================================================================================
Principal upstream projects
================================================================================

Narwhal composes these; it does not fork or modify them. Full per-image detail,
including the upstream repository each license was read from, is in
scripts/airgap/lib/component-licenses.tsv.

  Kubernetes, CoreDNS, etcd, kube-state-metrics, metrics-server, the CSI sidecars
    The Kubernetes Authors — Apache-2.0
  Cilium, Hubble, Istio, MetalLB, kube-vip, FRR
    networking and mesh — Apache-2.0 except FRR (GPL-2.0-or-later)
  Argo CD, Kyverno, cert-manager, Trivy Operator, Chaos Mesh, Falco
    delivery, policy and security — Apache-2.0
    (Falco's chart is in the bundle but its images are not in
    scripts/airgap/images.txt — see the scope caveat below.)
  Prometheus, Alertmanager, node-exporter, prometheus-operator, Grafana Alloy
    metrics — Apache-2.0
  Grafana, Loki, Tempo
    dashboards and logs/traces — AGPL-3.0 (see above)
  Keycloak, OpenBao, Dex
    identity and secrets — Apache-2.0, MPL-2.0, Apache-2.0
  Harbor, Gitea, SeaweedFS, CloudNativePG, Velero, APISIX, Valkey
    registry, git, storage, database, backup, gateway, cache — Apache-2.0
    except Gitea (MIT) and Valkey (BSD-3-Clause)

The Narwhal IDP Portal (ghcr.io/dasomel/narwhal-portal) is this project's own
work and is Apache-2.0; its dependency attribution lives in that repository.

================================================================================
Build and provisioning tooling
================================================================================

These are not redistributed — they are tools the operator runs — but their terms
are worth knowing because two of them are not open source at all.

  Vagrant, Packer                       BUSL-1.1 (HashiCorp)
    Business Source License, not an OSI license. Free for internal use; the
    grant excludes offering a competing product. GitHub cannot classify BUSL, so
    an automated scan reports these as unknown rather than flagging them — worth
    knowing before trusting such a report. Vagrant drives the local multi-VM
    cluster (Vagrantfile); nothing in a deployed cluster needs it.

  OpenTofu                              MPL-2.0
    Used for Kakao Cloud provisioning under csp/kakao-cloud/terraform/. The
    Terraform CLI also works there, but Terraform is BUSL-1.1 like the tools
    above; OpenTofu is the fork that stayed open source and is what the
    documented workflow uses.

  Vagrant box dasomel/ubuntu-26.04-xfs   MIT
    Built by the sibling project https://github.com/dasomel/kube-ready-box,
    whose own NOTICE covers the Ubuntu base and the packages inside it.

